## **I. Alloy Formation**

### **Core Theory**
- Alloys are **homogeneous solid solutions** where atoms of one metal are distributed randomly among the atoms of another metal.
- Such alloys are formed by atoms with metallic radii that are within about 15% of each other.
- The alloys so formed are hard and have often high melting points.
- Alloys of transition metals with non transition metals such as brass (copper-zinc) and bronze (copper-tin), are also of considerable industrial importance.

*   **D-Block Suitability:** Because transition metals in the same series have very similar atomic sizes, they form alloys extensively.
*   **Properties:** Alloys are generally **harder** and have **higher melting points** than their parent metals.

### **Common Examples Table**
| Alloy | Composition | Type |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Brass** | Copper (Cu) + Zinc (Zn) | Transition + Non-transition |
| **Bronze** | Copper (Cu) + Tin (Sn) | Transition + P-block |
| **Gun metal** | Cu(87%) + Sn(10%) + Zn(3%) |  |
| **Stainless Steel** | Fe + Cr + Ni + C | Ferrous Alloy |
| **German Silver** | Cu(50%) + Zn(25%) + Ni(25%) | Copper Alloy |
| **Bell metal** | Cu(80%) + Sn(20%) |  |
| **UK silvercoin** | Cu + Ni |  |

